Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Period live-action London, 1843.

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING HOUSE - DAY

Christmas Eve. Fog presses against the windows. The faded sign outside still reads SCROOGE AND MARLEY.

EBENEZER SCROOGE, sixties, gaunt and sharp-featured, wears severe black clothes over a stooped frame. Frost rims his wiry grey brows and chin.

In a cramped adjoining cell, BOB CRATCHIT, thirties, slight and threadbare, copies letters beside one dying coal. A long white comforter is his only defence against the cold.

FRED, Scrooge's ruddy, handsome nephew, enters glowing from the frost.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
What's Christmas time to you but a time for paying bills without money, finding yourself a year older and not an hour richer?

FRED
I have always thought of Christmas as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time. Though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe it has done me good. God bless it!

Bob applauds before remembering himself. His last coal collapses into ash.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Come! Dine with us tomorrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! And a happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves. MR. HOWELL, a portly gentleman carrying a subscription book, enters.

MR. HOWELL
At this festive season, it is desirable that we make some provision for the Poor and destitute. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

MR. HOWELL
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone.

MR. HOWELL
Many would rather die than enter the workhouse.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Mr. Howell closes his book and withdraws.

Night darkens the windows. Bob puts on his hat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day tomorrow, I suppose?

BOB
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

EXT. SCROOGE'S COURTYARD - NIGHT

Scrooge crosses the black, fog-bound yard toward his chambers.

He puts his key into the lock. The great brass knocker becomes the livid face of JACOB MARLEY, its spectacles raised upon its brow.

Scrooge recoils. The face becomes a knocker again.

SCROOGE
Humbug.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge, now in a dark dressing gown and nightcap, bends over a meagre fire and a bowl of gruel.

A disused bell begins to swing. Every bell in the house erupts.

Chains drag through the cellar, climb the stairs and stop outside.

The locked door bursts open. MARLEY'S GHOST enters through it: a transparent, middle-aged man in an old waistcoat, tights, boots and pigtail. A chain of cashboxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers and steel purses coils around him.

SCROOGE
What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much.

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese. There's more of gravy than of grave about you!

Marley shrieks and shakes his chain. He removes the bandage around his head, and his jaw drops upon his breast.

Scrooge falls to his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard. Is its pattern strange to you?

Scrooge searches the floor around himself.

MARLEY
You have laboured on your own chain since. It is a ponderous chain!

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY
Business! Mankind was my business. Charity, mercy, forbearance and benevolence were all my business!

Marley raises the crushing chain.

MARLEY
I am here tonight to warn you that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread.

The window rises. Marley floats backward into the night.

Outside, chained phantoms reach helplessly toward a poor mother and infant in the street below.

Scrooge slams the window and stumbles toward bed.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The clock strikes ONE. A burst of white light draws Scrooge's bed curtains apart.

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands there, child-sized yet ancient, with long white hair, a white tunic, summer flowers and winter holly. A clear jet of light shines from its crown.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit whose coming was foretold to me?

CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long past?

CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

The Spirit takes Scrooge's arm.

CHRISTMAS PAST
Rise, and walk with me.

INT. OLD SCHOOLROOM - DAY

A vast, decaying schoolroom. YOUNG EBENEZER, a thin, lonely boy, reads beside a feeble fire while snow fills the windows.

Scrooge sees his younger self and weeps.

SCROOGE
Poor boy.

His little sister FAN rushes in and embraces Young Ebenezer.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be.

YOUNG EBENEZER
Home, little Fan?

FAN
First, we're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world!

The children hurry away together.

CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had one child.

SCROOGE
My nephew.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

Warm lamps blaze over a Christmas ball. MR. FEZZIWIG, a round, elderly merchant in a Welsh wig and bright waistcoat, claps his hands.

FEZZIWIG
No more work tonight! Christmas Eve, Ebenezer! Clear away, my lads, and let's have lots of room here!

Young Ebenezer, now a cheerful apprentice, sweeps aside the desks. Fiddling begins. Workers dance while Fezziwig pours food, drink and welcome upon everyone.

Scrooge laughs, claps and dances unheard among the shadows.

CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds.

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy, to make our service a pleasure or a toil. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

The Spirit studies him.

SCROOGE
I should like to say a word or two to my clerk just now.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - NIGHT

YOUNG EBENEZER, now a tense young businessman, sits opposite BELLE, a gentle young woman in a plain mourning dress.

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG EBENEZER
What idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you.

YOUNG EBENEZER
I am not changed toward you.

BELLE
You are changed. I release you, with a full heart, for the love of him you once were. May you be happy in the life you have chosen.

Belle leaves. Young Ebenezer remains alone beside the dying fire.

SCROOGE
Spirit, show me no more! Why do you delight to torture me?

Scrooge seizes the Spirit's extinguisher-cap and forces it down. Light streams beneath it until everything disappears.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

A roaring fire has transformed the room into a grove of holly and ivy. Turkeys, puddings, fruit and steaming punch cover the floor.

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT, a broad, joyful giant with dark curls, sits upon the feast in a green robe trimmed with white fur.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in, and know me better, man!

SCROOGE
Conduct me where you will. If you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

INT. CRATCHIT PARLOUR - DAY

A poor four-roomed home made festive with cheap ribbons. MRS. CRATCHIT, forties, warm-faced in a twice-turned dress, lays out their small Christmas feast.

Bob enters carrying TINY TIM, a frail six-year-old boy with brown curls, a little crutch and iron braces on his legs.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB
As good as gold, and better. He hoped the people saw him in church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to remember upon Christmas Day who made lame beggars walk and blind men see.

The family gathers around one modest goose and a flaming pudding. Tiny Tim sits close beside Bob.

BOB
A merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

Scrooge watches Bob hold Tim's withered hand.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head.

Christmas Present opens his robe. Two ragged, wolfish children cling beneath it.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The clock strikes TWELVE. The Spirit vanishes.

EXT. LONDON EXCHANGE - DAY

A tall, silent GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME glides through the fog, entirely shrouded in black except for one outstretched hand.

Scrooge follows, trembling.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future, I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But I hope to live to be another man. Lead on.

The Phantom points to two prosperous merchants.

MR. GRAY
I only know he's dead.

MR. POLLARD
What has he done with his money?

MR. GRAY
I haven't heard. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral. I don't know anybody to go to it.

They laugh and walk away. Scrooge searches the Exchange but cannot find his future self.

INT. OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT

Rusty keys, bones, rags and chains surround OLD JOE, a grey-haired fence crouched beside a charcoal stove.

MRS. DILBER, a hard-faced charwoman, drops a bundle before him. He opens it to reveal bed-curtains, blankets and a fine shirt.

OLD JOE
You don't mean to say you took them down, rings and all, with him lying there?

MRS. DILBER
Yes, I do. Why not? If he had been natural in his lifetime, he'd have had somebody to look after him when he was struck with Death.

She snatches the coins Joe counts out.

MRS. DILBER
He frightened everyone away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

Scrooge recoils from their laughter.

SCROOGE
The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way now.

INT. DEAD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A bare bed stands in a plundered room. Beneath a ragged sheet lies a rigid body.

Rats gnaw under the hearthstone. The Phantom points to the covered face.

SCROOGE
This is a fearful place. In leaving it, I shall not leave its lesson. Let us go.

The finger remains fixed upon the face.

SCROOGE
I understand you, but I have not the power.

INT. CRATCHIT PARLOUR - DAY

The room is painfully quiet. Tiny Tim's empty stool stands beside the fire. His crutch rests against it.

Mrs. Cratchit sews through tears. Bob enters, moving more slowly than before.

BOB
I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child!

Bob breaks down. His family gathers around him.

BOB
When we recollect how patient and how mild he was, we shall not quarrel easily among ourselves, and forget poor Tiny Tim.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Never, father.

Scrooge turns desperately to the Phantom.

SCROOGE
Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

Weeds choke a neglected graveyard hemmed in by dark houses.

The Phantom points to one stone. Scrooge approaches and reads:

EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from the grave to Scrooge.

Scrooge falls to his knees and clutches the black robe.

SCROOGE
I am not the man I was! Why show me this if I am past all hope?

The spectral hand trembles.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present and the Future. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

The Phantom collapses into a wooden bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - MORNING

Sunlight floods the room. Scrooge embraces his intact bed-curtains, laughing and crying.

SCROOGE
They are here! I am here! The shadows of the things that would have been may be dispelled!

Church bells peal outside.

Scrooge throws open the window. SAM, a bright-eyed boy in Sunday clothes, stands below.

SCROOGE
What's today, my fine fellow?

SAM
Why, Christmas Day!

SCROOGE
I haven't missed it! Do you know whether they've sold the prize turkey in the next street?

SAM
The one as big as me? It's hanging there now.

SCROOGE
Go and buy it! Tell them to bring it here, and I'll give you half-a-crown!

Sam races away.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's. He shan't know who sends it!

EXT. LONDON STREET - DAY

Clear winter sunlight glitters on the snow. Scrooge, dressed in his best black coat, smiles at every passerby.

He spots Mr. Howell and takes both his hands.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, I hope you succeeded yesterday. Allow me to ask your pardon.

Scrooge whispers an amount into Mr. Howell's ear.

MR. HOWELL
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it.

MR. HOWELL
I don't know what to say to such munificence.

SCROOGE
Don't say anything. Come and see me.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - DAY

A Christmas table waits beneath warm lamps. Fred opens the door and stares.

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred seizes his uncle's hand and pulls him into a joyful embrace.

FRED
Let you in! A merry Christmas, uncle!

Scrooge laughs with him.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING HOUSE - DAY

The following morning. A large fire blazes.

Bob slips into his cell eighteen minutes late and attacks his work with frantic speed.

SCROOGE
What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB
I am very sorry, sir. It's only once a year. I was making rather merry yesterday.

SCROOGE
I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer.

Bob braces himself.

Scrooge leaps from his stool and claps him on the back.

SCROOGE
And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

BOB
Sir?

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas than I have given you for many a year. I'll raise your salary and endeavour to assist your struggling family.

Bob's eyes fill with tears.

SCROOGE
Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

INT. CRATCHIT PARLOUR - DAY

The room is warm and plentiful now. Tiny Tim, alive and smiling, walks between Bob and Scrooge with his crutch.

Scrooge steadies the boy like a second father. The whole family raises its glass.

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

Scrooge's own heart laughs with them.

FADE OUT.

THE END
